**/.vs
**/bin
**/obj
.claude/settings.local.json

# The browser-wasm archive is fetched, never committed.
#
# The other six runtimes are committed on purpose: CI checks that all 169 P/Invokes resolve
# against them, and a pull request does not fetch, so without them in the tree that check has
# nothing to read. That reasoning does not reach wasm. A static archive cannot be loaded to
# look up symbols, so the check skips it either way, and the file is the largest of the seven
# by a distance -- 12.8 MB per release, against 64 MB for all seven together.
#
# Committing it would cost roughly 0.8 GB a year of history, for a file no check reads and
# that git could never be made to forget. The CD fetches it from the CesiumC release the
# manifest records, which is the moment it is actually needed: packing.
Evergine.Bindings.CesiumNative/runtimes/browser-wasm/

# The iOS archives, for the same reason and ten times over.
#
# They are static archives like the wasm one, so the coherence check cannot load them to look up
# symbols and skips them either way. And they are far bigger: 60.9 MB for ios-arm64 and 61.0 MB
# for iossimulator-arm64 against wasm's 12.8 MB, because each carries cesium-native and its 143
# dependency archives merged in. At a release a month that is around 1.5 GB a year of history
# for two files nothing reads.
#
# They arrived committed because the first CD run after iOS was added fetched them and this rule
# did not exist yet. The CD fetches them from the CesiumC release the manifest records, which is
# the moment they are actually needed: packing.
Evergine.Bindings.CesiumNative/runtimes/ios-arm64/
Evergine.Bindings.CesiumNative/runtimes/iossimulator-arm64/

# The package under test is downloaded from a CD run, never committed. It is the whole
# 67 MB NuGet, including all seven native runtimes.
WasmSmokeTest/local-packages/
DesktopSmokeTest/local-packages/
